WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.601
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy- and Tony-nominated

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:05.267
and Academy Award-winning
actor and singer

00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:07.033
you know from her work
in "West Side Story,"

00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:08.734
"Schmigadoon!",
and "Hamilton" on Broadway.

00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:11.200
She stars in the new
animated movie "Wish,"

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.934
which is in theaters
nationwide on November 22nd.

00:00:13.934 --> 00:00:16.267
Please welcome to the show
Ariana DeBose, everybody.

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:18.501
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:42.033
-Whoo!

00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:45.467
Hey, y'all! Hi!

00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:49.767
-When someone like you,
who knows how to dance,

00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:51.567
the way you just walk
is so much --

00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:52.601
it makes me so jealous.

00:00:52.601 --> 00:00:53.868
When someone like you walks out,

00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:55.901
this is how I feel like
I walk out.

00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:59.701
-I mean, your timing,
though, is really good.

00:00:59.701 --> 00:01:01.567
-Thank you very much.
-Amazing.

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:02.868
-I'm so happy
to have you here.

00:01:02.868 --> 00:01:04.234
-I'm so happy to be here.

00:01:04.234 --> 00:01:06.400
-You have not been in this
building since you hosted

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.701
a wonderful episode of "SNL"
in January of '22, I believe?

00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:11.667
-I think that's correct, yeah.

00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:13.701
-Was it a special time
to be here for that?

00:01:13.701 --> 00:01:17.200
-It was.
I mean, why was I hosting "SNL"?

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.634
[ Laughs ]

00:01:19.634 --> 00:01:20.834
But it was so fun.

00:01:20.834 --> 00:01:23.000
And, I mean,
it's such an inherently

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.801
theatrical experience.

00:01:24.801 --> 00:01:26.968
-Yes.
-It's absolutely insane.

00:01:26.968 --> 00:01:30.667
And I don't know why people say
yes to it, but it's so fun.

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:35.234
And I it's funny,
because I feel a little nervy.

00:01:35.234 --> 00:01:36.968
Like, you can still feel the
nervous energy in the building.

00:01:36.968 --> 00:01:38.501
-I agree.
Like, sometimes for people

00:01:38.501 --> 00:01:40.200
who don't, like, come here
every day, they're like,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.667
"Oh, right, there was that very
scary thing down the hall."

00:01:42.667 --> 00:01:44.467
-Trauma bond, trauma bond.

00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:48.000
But it's so -- I don't know.
It's -- I love the show

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.501
And I love this place.
It's iconic.

00:01:50.501 --> 00:01:53.167
So anytime you get to be
in the space is great.

00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:55.734
-I -- For me, hosting "SNL"
was one of the most

00:01:55.734 --> 00:01:57.200
nerve-racking things I ever did.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.200
But I heard you -- And you did
such a wonderful job

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.501
hosting the Tonys.

00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:02.133
-Oh, thank you.
-Congratulations.

00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:04.934
-Thank you. I appreciate that.
Yeah.

00:02:04.934 --> 00:02:09.300
-And the timing of that
was also not ideal.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.367
It was right after
the writers' strike.

00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:14.234
You very happily received
a waiver from the Guild

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:16.467
to go on with the show,
which was wonderful to support

00:02:16.467 --> 00:02:17.634
all the incredible people
and the work they did that year.

00:02:17.634 --> 00:02:18.834
-Of course.

00:02:18.834 --> 00:02:21.934
-The opening
of the show was a dance number

00:02:21.934 --> 00:02:24.834
that was just incredible
to behold.

00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:25.834
-Thank you.

00:02:25.834 --> 00:02:27.767
-I mean,
the normal dancing stuff,

00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:29.300
which you do better
than anyone,

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.367
but you also jumped off
the top of the stairs.

00:02:31.367 --> 00:02:35.100
-Yes, I did that for Broadway.
-Yes.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.801
-No, that whole number
was kind of --

00:02:37.801 --> 00:02:40.934
I mean, of course it was born
of the moment we were in, right?

00:02:40.934 --> 00:02:44.801
And, so, I play to my strengths
when I'm in a tight spot.

00:02:44.801 --> 00:02:47.434
And I was like, "Cool, we're
going to do a dance overture

00:02:47.434 --> 00:02:49.167
and we're going
to celebrate music

00:02:49.167 --> 00:02:51.234
and we're going
to celebrate movement."

00:02:51.234 --> 00:02:56.467
And I pulled together a great
team, and we made it happen.

00:02:56.467 --> 00:03:00.234
And I have to say, though,

00:03:00.234 --> 00:03:04.634
that jump off the stairs wasn't
really rehearsed that way.

00:03:04.634 --> 00:03:06.334
-Yeah. Oh, wow.
-No. Like --

00:03:06.334 --> 00:03:08.033
-So, who did it different? You?

00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:09.901
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:03:09.901 --> 00:03:11.234
-Like I -- You know --

00:03:11.234 --> 00:03:13.167
-Because I should note
that someone caught you,

00:03:13.167 --> 00:03:15.300
so I would hope --
That would be the only person

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.868
I would think
you'd want to rehearse with.

00:03:16.868 --> 00:03:18.601
-Oh, no, of course. But we did.

00:03:18.601 --> 00:03:21.300
So, Julius Anthony Rubio
is a dear, dear friend of mine,

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.501
and he was one of the dancers
in "West Side Story."

00:03:23.501 --> 00:03:25.200
And so when I decided
I was going to

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.567
jump down the stairs,
I was like, "Cool, cool.

00:03:26.567 --> 00:03:28.434
You gonna catch me."
So...

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:30.033
-Is he, like,
better at catching than most?

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:31.367
-Oh, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:31.367 --> 00:03:34.434
-I mean, he is -- He's the guy
you want to catch you.

00:03:34.434 --> 00:03:37.968
And I had rehearsed
safety first, you know,

00:03:37.968 --> 00:03:40.601
so you go up and back, right?

00:03:40.601 --> 00:03:45.634
Simple. I had so much adrenaline
in my body, I just said, "Boom!"

00:03:45.634 --> 00:03:46.968
-You really did.

00:03:46.968 --> 00:03:49.067
-It was --
So, to be perfectly frank,

00:03:49.067 --> 00:03:51.567
that moment is kind of
like TV magic,

00:03:51.567 --> 00:03:53.634
because I wasn't
supposed to do that.

00:03:53.634 --> 00:03:55.467
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:55.467 --> 00:03:57.634
-The crazy thing is,
I was watching and I thought,

00:03:57.634 --> 00:03:58.801
"God, look at that.

00:03:58.801 --> 00:04:00.334
She's probably done
that a thousand times,

00:04:00.334 --> 00:04:02.334
and she makes it look
like the first time ever."

00:04:02.334 --> 00:04:03.567
-Oh!

00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:05.601
-And now I'm finding out,
oh, first time ever.

00:04:05.601 --> 00:04:08.567
There you go.
-So fresh. It was so fresh.

00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:10.267
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:12.334
-I definitely --
With the writers on strike,

00:04:12.334 --> 00:04:14.100
I watched that incredible
dance overture and I'm like,

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.667
"Oh, we don't need
writers, yeah."

00:04:15.667 --> 00:04:17.234
-Well -- No, no, no, no.

00:04:17.234 --> 00:04:19.467
I love my writers.
Most everything I do --

00:04:19.467 --> 00:04:21.167
-I'm joking. I'm joking.
-I know.

00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:23.467
But I feel like there
are many writers

00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:25.133
who would kill me
if I didn't say this.

00:04:25.133 --> 00:04:27.968
And I am here because
of some incredible writers.

00:04:27.968 --> 00:04:29.634
Tony Kushner being one of them.
So shout-out, Tony Kushner.

00:04:29.634 --> 00:04:30.734
-Yeah, there you go.
Shout-out to Tony Kushner.

00:04:30.734 --> 00:04:31.868
-Yeah.
-One of the all time greats.

00:04:31.868 --> 00:04:33.234
-Yeah.

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:35.968
-You've talked about your mom
many times in the past.

00:04:35.968 --> 00:04:38.801
Does she -- I'm assuming
on an evening like the Tonys,

00:04:38.801 --> 00:04:40.200
is she there?

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.968
Is she supporting you?
Is she going into the parties?

00:04:41.968 --> 00:04:43.767
-Well, you know,
this particular year,

00:04:43.767 --> 00:04:45.133
this past year,
she was not there.

00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:47.801
But, normally, my mom
is my date for everything.

00:04:47.801 --> 00:04:50.167
She came to the Oscars.

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:53.267
She was my -- I think
my favorite memory of my mom

00:04:53.267 --> 00:04:55.968
is at what I call
the "Hamil-Tony Party."

00:04:55.968 --> 00:04:57.300
So it was the year
that "Hamilton"

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.100
was honored with
many, many things.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:04.167
And she literally
was just having the best time.

00:05:04.167 --> 00:05:07.434
I looked over, and she was
cutting a rug with Common.

00:05:07.434 --> 00:05:09.267
-Oh, wow.
-Like, amazing.

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:12.567
I was like, "Mom,
did you just hit on Common?"

00:05:12.567 --> 00:05:13.934
Like -- And then
Mariska Hargitay was,

00:05:13.934 --> 00:05:15.834
like, jammin' behind her,

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:17.334
and I was like,
"What is happening?"

00:05:17.334 --> 00:05:20.100
But moms and famous people
are the best.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:25.868
-My mom danced with Idris Elba
once at an Emmy party,

00:05:25.868 --> 00:05:29.100
and my dad still
can't watch "The Wire," yeah.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.634
It's too hard for him now.
It's too hard for him.

00:05:31.634 --> 00:05:32.667
-But come on.

00:05:32.667 --> 00:05:34.234
I mean,
the entertainment industry

00:05:34.234 --> 00:05:36.133
is filled with beautiful people,
and so sometimes,

00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:38.467
I'm just like,
"Yeah, Mom, go get yours."

00:05:38.467 --> 00:05:39.834
-And you know what? Can I say?

00:05:39.834 --> 00:05:42.701
I want to give a tip of my cap
to the beautiful people

00:05:42.701 --> 00:05:44.534
who every now and then
appreciate the moment.

00:05:44.534 --> 00:05:45.901
They're like, "You know what?

00:05:45.901 --> 00:05:47.901
I'm going to make someone's mom
feel good right now."

00:05:47.901 --> 00:05:50.033
And so I have a very
special place in my heart

00:05:50.033 --> 00:05:51.467
for Idris Elba for that moment.

00:05:51.467 --> 00:05:53.234
And I feel like Common,
as well, right?

00:05:53.234 --> 00:05:57.467
-Absolutely. Shout-out to both.
-Shout-out to both of you.

00:05:57.467 --> 00:05:59.868
I have a lot more to ask
about your exciting new film.

00:05:59.868 --> 00:06:02.133
We'll be right back
with Ariana after this.

